This folder contains two different  scripts:

DNAzyme_scan.pl
===============
This is a PERL program that takes as an input a FASTA file with RNA (or DNA) sequences and reports all putative target sites with their respective DNAzymes, together with the predicted binding free energy (DeltaG). To run it with the provided example file the command is:

perl DNAzyme_scan.pl -i HPV_16 -o DNAzymes_HPV_16

Optionally, the user can specify the arm length (set by default to 9 nt) with the option '-l':

perl DNAzyme_scan.pl  -i HPV_16 -o DNAzymes_HPV_16 -l 10

This script is free to use and distribute for non-commercial purposes. Please read the file COPYING for further information.


run_analysis_DNAzymes.R
=======================
This is a R script containing the code to reproduce all the statistical analysis described in the main paper. It also produces figures 2 and 3. The default input file is also provided as 'HPV_FullLength.tab'


Screening DNAzymes for your own target
======================================
The two scripts above cover enumeration and the published statistical analysis.
Two helper scripts bridge the gap between them, so that a FASTA of any transcript
can be turned into a shortlist worth ordering:

  perl   DNAzyme_scan.pl     -i MYTARGET.fa -o scan.tab
  perl   screen_dnazymes.pl  -i scan.tab    -o shortlist
  python compute_features.py shortlist_dnazymes.fa -o features.tab

screen_dnazymes.pl (added) ranks the candidates from DNAzyme_scan.pl and drops the
ones that are impractical to synthesise: per-arm GC outside 20-80%, runs of more
than five identical bases, and weakly binding arms. It writes the full ranked table
plus a FASTA of the survivors.

compute_features.py (added) recomputes the thermodynamic features with the ViennaRNA
Package (pip install ViennaRNA), using the same energy set as the paper, i.e. the
Mathews 2004 DNA parameters with --noLP. It needs no external parameter file.

Why the extra step: the DeltaG reported by DNAzyme_scan.pl is the nearest-neighbour
energy of the arms hybridised to a perfectly accessible target. It ignores the
folding of the DNAzyme itself, the secondary structure of the target site, and the
catalytic step. Measured against the 38 HPV16 DNAzymes with published cleavage data
in HPV_FullLength.tab, that DeltaG does not separate good from poor DNAzymes at all
(AUC 0.48, i.e. chance), whereas the intramolecular folding energy of the DNAzyme
does (AUC 0.67). Ordering the 38 by folding energy puts 7 of the 11 good ones in the
top 15; ordering them by DeltaG puts only 4 of 11. compute_features.py reproduces the
'Internal' column of HPV_FullLength.tab exactly (maximum deviation 0.00 kcal/mol),
which is the check that its parameters match those used in the paper.

Both helpers are provided under the GPLv3 license, like run_analysis_DNAzymes.R, and
are not subject to the CC BY-NC-ND terms that apply to DNAzyme_scan.pl.


